Two independent defects in OpenStax's own source MathML for this page, not
converter gaps -- the converter faithfully reproduces what's in the source
in both cases:

1. Exercise "y = x^4, x = 0, and y = 1 for x >= 0": every other "and"/word
   join in this exercise set is preceded by an explicit <mspace width="0.2em"/>,
   but the source omits the <mspace> before the <mtext>for</mtext> token
   here, so "1" and "for" glue together as "1for" with no separating space.
   Restored the space by hand (\, before \text{for}) to match the spacing
   convention every sibling exercise already uses.

2. Exercise "y = 1/(x+1), x = 0, x = 2, and y = 0": the source has a stray
   duplicate <mo>,</mo> with an empty <mspace/> on either side and nothing
   between the two commas (<mo>,</mo><mspace/><mo>,</mo><mspace/><mi>x</mi>),
   producing a literal double comma "x=0, , x=2" with nothing between them.
   Removed the extra, contentless comma.

A third, unrelated cleanup found during manual review: nearly every
exercise in this section names the x-axis/y-axis in plain prose (e.g. "the
solid is rotated around the x-axis"), which is a plain-text `<p>` element
in the source rather than a `<math>` span, so the extractor never has a
chance to mathify the bare letter. Every other file across this whole
openstax/ corpus that mentions the x-/y-axis in prose uses `$x$-axis`/
`$y$-axis`, matching the skill's own stated convention -- this page's
"x-axis"/"y-axis" (and one bare "x axis" typo, and this page's own
inconsistent one-off `$x\text{-axis}$`/`$y\text{-axis}$` form) were
normalized to `$x$-axis`/`$y$-axis` to match. Also fixed a stray lowercase
sentence start ("...coordinate system. the radius...") and mathified the
bare "xyz" in "xyz coordinate system" to `$xyz$` to match `$xyz$-plane`/
`$xyz$-space` elsewhere in the corpus.

